  ],
  "text": "Earth is closest to the Sun in January, right in the middle of winter. Hold your hand near a hot stove. Closer always means warmer. But Earth's orbit is almost a perfect circle, so the distance barely changes. What changes is the tilt, twenty three degrees, and it never straightens up. That lean aims sunlight straight down on one side, low and thin on the other. Spread the same sunlight over more ground, and every patch gets less heat. The tilted side also gets fewer hours of daylight, so cold stacks on cold. Right now, the other hemisphere is having summer. Same day. Same distance. Opposite season. So the calendar was never about distance. It's about which way the planet leans. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
